- op: discuss  # dcda
  at: '2026-07-30T16:48:23Z'  # dcda
  by: agent  # dcda
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dcda
  clock: 3  # dcda
  nonce: dcda  # dcda
  message: "DONE — PR merged to dev. Fixed at the registry, not per-key.\n\nMEASURED before: docs/schema.json described 2 of 79 registered meta keys — Edge.meta hand-listed 2 of 32 edge-axis keys, Symbol.meta 0 of 47 (it is a bare Dict[str, Any], so the generator emitted a contentless {\"type\": \"object\"}). resolution_quality is on 1076 self-corpus edges and was absent; channel is on 145 (WI-zamum, same root, closed together).\n\nAFTER: both meta blocks derive their properties from axis_meta_keys via generate_schema_lib.registry_meta_properties. Edge.meta 2 -> 34, Symbol.meta 0 -> 47. A newly registered key now documents itself instead of becoming the next filed item.\n\nDESIGN POINT worth keeping: entries carry a description and deliberately NO type. MetaKeySpec records no type, and the values are genuinely mixed — refresh and disambiguation_fallback are booleans, referring_paths is a list, most are strings. Emitting \"type\": \"string\" across all 79 would have converted a documentation gap into a WRONG CONSTRAINT. additionalProperties stays open on both blocks, so this changes no validation outcome and no SCHEMA_VERSION bump is owed. The two curated entries keep their richer hand-written text (evidence_type carries the ADR-0028 axiom + the x-axis-of-values map, which the registry does not).\n\nTwo ratchets in test_schema.py: every registered key must appear in the schema for its axis (registering without regenerating fails there, not later), and registry-derived entries must stay description-only with additionalProperties open, so documenting can never silently become a closed whitelist."  # dcda
